WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.416
Third video for week one.

2
00:00:01.466 --> 00:00:03.470
The first video gave you the loop.

3
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:10.509
This one names the parts around the loop, because that is what you will build, test and defend for the rest of the course.

4
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.976
We call it the harness.

5
00:00:12.726 --> 00:00:13.950
Here is the picture.

6
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.860
The model sits in the middle.

7
00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:30.185
Everything around it is the harness: the tools it can call, the context it sees on each turn, the conditions that end the loop, the gates where a person must say yes, and the trace, which is the record of every call and every result.

8
00:00:30.235 --> 00:00:34.006
In the first lab you will write or configure each of these.

9
00:00:34.056 --> 00:00:36.234
The word harness is borrowed from testing.

10
00:00:36.284 --> 00:00:40.691
A test harness holds the thing under test and controls what it can touch.

11
00:00:40.741 --> 00:00:43.556
An agent harness does the same for a model.

12
00:00:44.306 --> 00:00:46.328
Why does this matter so much?

13
00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:50.473
Because two agents can use the same model and behave completely differently.

14
00:00:50.523 --> 00:00:57.726
A demo gives the model any tool at any time, runs until it feels done, keeps no record, and asks nobody.

15
00:00:57.776 --> 00:01:08.778
A service gives the model a short list of tools with limits, a budget of steps and a definition of done, a trace of every call, and a gate before anything irreversible.

16
00:01:08.828 --> 00:01:12.577
The Replit incident from the first video was a harness failure.

17
00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:20.175
The model had a tool that could delete a production database, during a code freeze, with no gate in front of it.

18
00:01:20.225 --> 00:01:27.083
The fixes the company announced afterwards were harness work: separate development and production databases, a planning-only mode, and one-click restore.

19
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:30.582
One more distinction you will use every week.

20
00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:34.780
Anthropic's essay Building effective agents, from December 2024, separates workflows from agents.

21
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:43.176
In a workflow the steps are fixed in code and the model fills in each step: classify this, then draft that, then check it.

22
00:01:43.226 --> 00:01:47.024
In an agent the model decides the steps as it goes.

23
00:01:47.074 --> 00:01:49.473
Workflows are predictable and cheap to test.

24
00:01:49.523 --> 00:01:54.371
Agents handle cases nobody wrote down, at the price of being harder to predict.

25
00:01:54.421 --> 00:02:03.117
The essay's advice is to start with the simplest design that does the job, and many production systems are workflows with a small agent inside.

26
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.916
Treat that as the default for your project.

27
00:02:06.666 --> 00:02:08.612
So the harness decides five things.

28
00:02:08.662 --> 00:02:10.941
Which tools exist and with what permissions.

29
00:02:10.991 --> 00:02:15.266
What the model sees each turn, and what it is kept away from.

30
00:02:15.316 --> 00:02:22.919
When to stop, whether because the job is done, a step budget ran out, a cost budget ran out, or an error occurred.

31
00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:26.246
What needs a human, and how the human is asked.

32
00:02:26.296 --> 00:02:30.238
And what is recorded, so that someone can read afterwards what happened.

33
00:02:30.288 --> 00:02:35.561
Each of those is a design decision, and each will appear in one of your deliverables.

34
00:02:35.611 --> 00:02:38.556
The security review is mostly about the first two.

35
00:02:38.606 --> 00:02:41.217
The evaluation report is about reading the record.

36
00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.213
The operations sheet is about budgets.

37
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.790
This is not a beginner's topic that goes away.

38
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.223
In November 2025 Anthropic published a piece on harnesses for agents that run for hours or days.

39
00:02:52.273 --> 00:02:57.337
Those agents work in sessions, and each session starts with no memory of the last one.

40
00:02:57.387 --> 00:02:59.574
The answer was not a bigger model.

41
00:02:59.624 --> 00:03:07.883
It was harness work: a progress file the agent updates, a task list it works through, and a clean checkpoint at the end of every session.

42
00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:13.956
In week two you will build your own harness and expose it so that other software can call it.

43
00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:22.265
Before then, run the first lab: a sixty-line loop with two tools, or the same loop in Fleet, and read one trace from start to finish.

44
00:03:22.315 --> 00:03:26.420
Notice what the harness let the model do, and what it did not.
